{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-18_14-39-49-dragonboat_raft-real-run && python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('state.json'))\niv=[x for x in s['interpretations'] if x['id'].startswith('0c41b53c')][0]\nprint(iv['rationale'])\nprint('=== JOINT')\nprint(iv['joint_history'])\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-18_14-39-49-dragonboat_raft-real-run && python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('state.json'))\niv=[x for x in s['interpretations'] if x['id'].startswith('0c41b53c')][0]\nprint(iv['rationale'])\nprint('=== JOINT')\nprint(iv['joint_history'])\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"The conclusion remains a violated requirement and a bounded client-completion defect for remote ReadIndex requests whenever the leader confirms a later queued context before an earlier one. The source cause is unchanged: handleReadIndexLeaderConfirmation returns every earlier queued request but builds each remote reply from the confirming heartbeat's Hint and HintHigh instead of that request's s.ctx, while readIndex.confirm deletes the earlier queue entry and the receiving node's pendingReadIndex associates a batch only when the response SystemCtx equals the registered key. That ordering needs no artificial message reordering: while a read is pending, the leader's periodic heartbeat carries the newest queued context because peepCtx returns the last queued context, followers echo the hint, and handleLeaderHeartbeatResp confirms it, so a confirmation produced by a periodic heartbeat confirms the newest pending request and returns every earlier queued request under that context. The receipts cover three requesters and both a harness-chosen and a tick-driven confirmation. In the follower receipt, node 2's admitted context {101,1002} is answered with node 3's later context {202,2003} at log index 4, and node 2's batch stays at index zero after the real ready index 4 is applied; the late confirmation of node 2's own context produces no context-correct reply. The logical-deadline receipt keeps that ordering and shows no completion before the 50-tick deadline, RequestTimeout at tick 51, and removal of the stale batch. The observer-endpoint receipt closes the link that the earlier leader-side observer receipt left open: a real observer peer forwards its own generated context to the real leader, voter 3's later context is confirmed first, the reply addressed to observer 4 carries voter 3's context at log index 4, the observer's real handler emits a ReadyToRead carrying that later context at index 4, and the observer's admitted batch stays at index zero without completing, even after the late own-context confirmation. The tick-driven receipt shows the same substitution under ordinary operation instead of harness-selected delivery: with two queued follower reads, one leader tick makes the periodic heartbeat carry the newest context, the reply to the earlier requester carries that newest context at index 4, and its batch stays at index zero. The controls complete when the admitted context is confirmed first or is the only queued context: the follower control's reply carries its context and its read completes at batch index 4, the observer control's reply carries the observer's context and its read completes, and the single-read tick control carries that read's own context and completes at batch index 4. Because every measured reply carries a later committed index and the ready index equals the applied index, no stale read value or safety violation is established; the established consequence is that an affected client read cannot complete until its deadline even though the leader had a completed confirmation and the node applied a valid ready index. The observer case shows the defect reaches a non-voting client endpoint through the observer's own forwarding and consumer rather than only the leader's outbound message.\n=== JOINT\nThe follower, sequential-control, and logical-deadline receipts come from the same three-peer in-process harness family; the leader-side observer receipt uses real internal Raft handlers for voters {1,2,3} with observer 4; the observer-endpoint receipt pairs the same three-peer leader with a real observer raft peer plus the node-level pending-read registry; the tick-driven receipt reuses that same harness file from the observer-endpoint experiment and adds a periodic-heartbeat case with its own control. All six experiments sit under the same ReadIndex question and exercise the same leader-side confirmation path. The follower case and the observer, tick-driven and timeout cases queue an earlier remote context before a later voter context and confirm the later one first; the follower and observer controls confirm the admitted context first, and the tick control queues a single read. The observer-endpoint receipt supplies the observer local consumer and completion that the leader-side receipt left unmeasured, and the tick-driven receipt supplies the periodic-heartbeat confirmation that the earlier accounts had only traced from source.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
